  "query_preview": "..(Friday through Wednesday)\nIssued at 228 PM EDT WED AUG 19 2026\n\nSome showers and storms will be possible at the start of the \nextended period ahead of a sagging cold front. The primary \ncorridor for pre-frontal convection will initially reside along \nand south of the Hal Rogers Parkway/KY-80 corridor toward the \nTennessee border. Aloft, the broad pattern features a ridge over \nthe western CONUS and a trough over the eastern CONUS. With broad \ntroughing modeled over the Great Lakes/Northeast and a distinct \nshortwave axis extending south- southwestward through OH/IN/KY, \nmid-level forcing will lag behind the surface boundary. This \nlingering PVA combined with steepening mid-level lapse rates may \nsupport isolated to scattered post-frontal convection Friday \nafternoon, with the greatest chances remaining east of the \nPottsville Escarpment. Given weak ambient shear, any activity that\ndevelops should remain pulse-like. While PWATs remain elevated \nbetween 1.3 and 1.6 inches (50th to 75th percentile) into Friday \nevening, significant mid-level drying will filter in from the \nnorthwest, as reflected by modeled 700 mb RH dropping into the \nteens. Clearing skies, calm winds, and strong subsidence/capping \nunder high pressure will favor areas of fog overnight Friday, \nparticularly within the river valleys.\n\nAn embedded shortwave within the longwave trough is modeled to \ndig out of northwestern Canada into the Great Lakes and Ohio \nValley over the weekend. A reinforcing cold front will cross \nKentucky, though timing discrepancies maintain uncertainty in \nPOPs. Regardless, precipitation chances should remain isolated, \nwith the highest potential staying northeast of the forecast area.\n\nHigh pressure briefly builds across eastern Kentucky early next \nweek, ushering in a cooler and drier airmass. Dew points lowering \ninto the upper 50s and lower 60s will be a welcome relief from \nrecent values in the upper 60s to lower 70s. Dry weather persists \nthrough Tuesday before another shortwave propagates out of the \nUpper Midwest into the region mid-week, bringing isolated to \nscattered shower and thunderstorm chances back to the forecast.",
